issue_context.body:
A the moment we don't have the ability to handle sig alg OIDs that we don't already know about. This means that you can't have an X509 certificate chain where intermediate or root CA have public keys that we don't already know - because we would not recognise the OID for their signature. This also means you couldn't have a self-signed cert using an algorithm we don't already know.

We actually already have the ability for application code to register their own sig alg OIDs via `OBJ_add_sigid` and `OBJ_create`

So, a provider that was linked to the same instance of libcrypto as the application could do something like the following in its `OSSL_provider_init` function:

````
int mdnid = OBJ_create("1.2.3.4", "myhash", "myhash");
int signid = OBJ_create("1.2.3.5", "mysig", "mysig");
int sigalgnid = OBJ_create("1.2.3.6", "mysigalg", "mysigalg");

OBJ_add_sigid(sigalgnid, mdnid, signid);
````

AFAICT that would be sufficient (untested) for a provider to register a new sigalg and an application could use it anywhere it likes in a cert chain.

The above fails if the provider isn't linked against the same instance of libcrypto (e.g. if static linking is being used). It could easily be wrapped up in an upcall though. Should we add that?

comments:
What I meant, btw, is an added API to be able to use fetched EVP_SIGNATUREs directly and explicitly.

So no, it doesn't mean both, it really means that OBJ_find_sigid_algs and OBJ_find_sigid_by_algs can (and I'm of the opinion that they should) be made obsolete.

So, simply put, something like this would be suitable:

``` C
EVP_PKEY_CTX *ctx = EVP_PKEY_CTX_new(pkey, NULL);
EVP_SIGNATURE *sig = EVP_SIGNATURE_fetch(libctx, "sha1WithRSAEncryption", propq);

EVP_SIGNATURE_init(ctx, sig, params);
EVP_SIGNATURE_update(ctx, data, datalen);
EVP_SIGNATURE_final(ctx, sigbuf, &sigbuflen, sigbufsize);
```

There's no *real* reason why anyone should *have to* do the split-oid-into-two-ids-and-rejoin-them dance.
And of course, that fetch works with un-named OIDs as well, so the fetch above can as well be:

``` C
EVP_SIGNATURE *sig = EVP_SIGNATURE_fetch(libctx, "1.2.840.113549.1.1.5", propq);
```

... as long as this is part of the algorithm names given in the OSSL_ALGORITHM.
